          <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> About the project</span>
          <h2>From manual checks to <span class="grad-text">continuous care.</span></h2>
          <p>Traditional pond monitoring depends on someone physically visiting the pond, testing a sample, and reacting after the fact — by which point stressed or dying fish are often the first sign anything was wrong. It's slow, inconsistent, and impossible to scale across multiple ponds or hatchery tanks.</p>
          <p><strong>BlueCycle solves this by continuously monitoring water quality using IoT sensors and automatically maintaining safe pond conditions</strong>, day and night, without waiting for a human to notice a problem. The completed prototype uses an <strong>ESP32</strong> as the main controller and local WiFi dashboard host, paired with an <strong>Arduino UNO</strong> acting as a dedicated pH-reading bridge over I2C for a cleaner, more stable measurement.</p>
          <ul class="about-list">
            <li><span class="tick"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#04241c" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></span>Built as a working small-scale prototype, not a concept — every sensor and actuator described here was wired, coded and demonstrated live.</li>
            <li><span class="tick"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#04241c" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></span>Reacts on its own — relay-driven pumps, buzzer alerts and a scheduled servo feeder respond to sensor thresholds without operator input.</li>
            <li><span class="tick"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#04241c" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></span>Won 1st place at the university Project Show, judged against every other final-year electronics and IoT build.</li>
