  <!-- ============================== FEATURE DETAILS ============================== -->
  <section id="feature-details" class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-block center">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Feature details</span>
        <h2>How each response actually works</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-detail reveal">
        <div class="fd-visual"><span class="fd-tag">DS18B20</span><span class="fd-index">01</span>
          <svg width="120" height="120" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0B3D91" stroke-width="1.6"><path d="M12 3v10.5a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-2 0V3h2Z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="fd-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Feature 01</span>
          <h3>Temperature monitoring</h3>
          <p>A waterproof DS18B20 digital sensor sits in the pond and reports live water temperature to the ESP32 over its one-wire bus.</p>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h10"/></svg></div><div><h5>Working principle</h5><p>Readings are compared against a safe band of roughly 18°C–32°C; the ESP32 flags a temperature alert badge the moment a reading leaves that range.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 3c3 4 6 7.5 6 11a6 6 0 1 1-12 0c0-3.5 3-7 6-11Z"/></svg></div><div><h5>Real-life benefit</h5><p>High water temperature lowers dissolved oxygen — catching it early lets the system aerate before fish are stressed.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-detail reverse reveal">
        <div class="fd-visual"><span class="fd-tag">Arduino UNO · I2C</span><span class="fd-index">02</span>
          <svg width="120" height="120" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0B3D91" stroke-width="1.6"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8"/><path d="M12 7v5l3 2"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="fd-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Feature 02</span>
          <h3>pH monitoring &amp; dosing</h3>
          <p>An analog pH probe is read by a dedicated Arduino UNO, which averages the signal, converts it against a calibrated neutral baseline, and streams the result to the ESP32 as a slave device over I2C.</p>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M8 7h8l1 13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H8a1 1 0 0 1-1-1L8 7Z"/><path d="M9 3h6l1 4H8l1-4Z"/></svg></div><div><h5>Working principle</h5><p>The safe band is treated as pH 6.5–8.5. Below it, water reads acidic and the base pump can respond; above it, water reads basic and the acid pump can respond.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 2 3 7v6c0 5 4 8 9 9 5-1 9-4 9-9V7l-9-5Z"/></svg></div><div><h5>Real-life benefit</h5><p>Offloading pH reading to a second microcontroller removed the analog noise that made ESP32-only readings unreliable — a real fix found during testing.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-detail reveal">
        <div class="fd-visual"><span class="fd-tag">Analog · Calibrated</span><span class="fd-index">03</span>
          <svg width="120" height="120" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0B3D91" stroke-width="1.6"><path d="M4 15c2 2 4 2 6 0s4-2 6 0 4 2 4 2M4 19c2 2 4 2 6 0s4-2 6 0 4 2 4 2"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="fd-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Feature 03</span>
          <h3>Turbidity monitoring</h3>
          <p>A turbidity sensor is calibrated against a normal-pond-water baseline, then watched for drift that signals cloudy or dirty water.</p>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h10"/></svg></div><div><h5>Working principle</h5><p>Raw readings are compared to a calibrated baseline and sensitivity range; crossing the threshold flips the dashboard status from clean to dirty.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 3c3 4 6 7.5 6 11a6 6 0 1 1-12 0c0-3.5 3-7 6-11Z"/></svg></div><div><h5>Real-life benefit</h5><p>Cloudy water is often an early sign of overfeeding or waste build-up — flagging it early supports timely water exchange.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-detail reverse reveal">
        <div class="fd-visual"><span class="fd-tag">HC-SR04</span><span class="fd-index">04</span>
          <svg width="120" height="120" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0B3D91" stroke-width="1.6"><path d="M12 3v3M8 21h8M12 6a6 6 0 0 0-3 11.2V21h6v-3.8A6 6 0 0 0 12 6Z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="fd-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Feature 04</span>
          <h3>Water level monitoring</h3>
          <p>An ultrasonic sensor measures the distance from itself down to the water surface, with a safe center point around 15&nbsp;cm.</p>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h10"/></svg></div><div><h5>Working principle</h5><p>Below ~14 cm reads as overflow (display only); above ~16 cm reads as underflow and switches the water pump on automatically in auto mode.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 2 3 7v6c0 5 4 8 9 9 5-1 9-4 9-9V7l-9-5Z"/></svg></div><div><h5>Real-life benefit</h5><p>Keeps pond depth in the range fish need without anyone manually checking a level marker.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-detail reveal">
        <div class="fd-visual"><span class="fd-tag">MG90S Servo</span><span class="fd-index">05</span>
          <svg width="120" height="120" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0B3D91" stroke-width="1.6"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/><path d="M12 3v2M12 19v2M3 12h2M19 12h2M5.6 5.6l1.4 1.4M17 17l1.4 1.4M18.4 5.6L17 7M7 17l-1.4 1.4"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="fd-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Feature 05</span>
          <h3>Automatic feeding</h3>
          <p>Two feeding times are set from the dashboard. When either arrives, a 360° servo turns to release food, then reverses after a set delay.</p>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h10"/></svg></div><div><h5>Working principle</h5><p>Home and target limit switches confirm the feeder has actually opened and returned, so a feeding cycle can't stall mid-way.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 2 3 7v6c0 5 4 8 9 9 5-1 9-4 9-9V7l-9-5Z"/></svg></div><div><h5>Real-life benefit</h5><p>Consistent, on-time feeding without staff being physically present — a direct labour saving for a working farm.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-detail reverse reveal">
        <div class="fd-visual"><span class="fd-tag">4-Ch Relay</span><span class="fd-index">06</span>
          <svg width="120" height="120" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#0B3D91" stroke-width="1.6"><rect x="5" y="7" width="14" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M9 12h6"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="fd-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Feature 06</span>
          <h3>Automatic pump control</h3>
          <p>Acid, base, air and water pumps are each switched through their own channel on a 4-channel optocoupler-isolated relay module.</p>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h10"/></svg></div><div><h5>Working principle</h5><p>The ESP32 decision layer switches each relay HIGH or LOW based on live sensor thresholds, with a short cooldown between automatic actions to avoid rapid re-triggering.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fd-subblock"><div class="fd-ico"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 2 3 7v6c0 5 4 8 9 9 5-1 9-4 9-9V7l-9-5Z"/></svg></div><div><h5>Real-life benefit</h5><p>Keeps higher-current pump loads electrically isolated from the microcontroller — the exact fix used after an early ESP32 was damaged during testing.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================== SYSTEM WORKFLOW ============================== -->
  <section id="workflow" class="section" style="background:var(--bg-alt);">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head-block center">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> System workflow</span>
        <h2>Sensor to decision to action</h2>
        <p>The completed system follows a sensor → controller → output architecture, closing the loop automatically before it reaches the dashboard.</p>
